\section{Data}
 A final feature of this data set is that the years of the panel span 
an important and dramatic policy change for education in Wisconsin - the passage of 
Wisconsin Act 10 by Wisconsin Governor Scott Walker. While the political fallout 
over this legislation was nightly news across the nation, the implications of Act 10
were and continue to be particularly salient for school districts, school boards, 
and local policy regarding the governance of education in the state. Combined with 
unprecedented cuts in per-pupil-spending, Act 10 was framed as a set of "tools" 
to give local officials - elected school boards - the flexibility to reduce their 
fixed costs by renegotiating employee compensation and benefits with employees now 
with much weakened labor representation and substantial limits on their bargaining 
authority. \footnote{Perhaps the most salient 
issue is the requirement under the law that any wage increase greater than a cost 
of living adjustment pegged to CPI be approved by a district wide referenda - 
effectively placing a firm ceiling on any salary negotiations2011 Wisconsin Act 10: 
https://docs.legis.wisconsin.gov/2011/related/acts/10}


\section{Methods}

\subsection{Causal Inference Using Natural Experiments}

The approach here is to leverage the passage of Act 10 as an exogenous shock which 
allows us to reasonably assume that differences between the period prior to the shock 
and after the shock are causally attributable to the shock itself. 

\subsection{Candidacy}

Combining this information with returns from the late spring recall election of 
the Governor--certain to be a statewide referendum on the Governor's education policies--it is 
possible using data from all 424 school districts in the state to directly to test 
whether divided communities see greater participation by challenger candidates than 
unified communities. This would represent the first comprehensive test of the public 
choice theory of school board elections and represent the first test of Dissatisfaction 
Theory across an entire state using actual election results instead of self-reports 
of school board members and superintendents \citep{RefWorks:243,RefWorks:297}. The 
empirical model of candidate emergence (CE) would look something like: \\

\[ (2) \hspace{0.25 in}  CE_{t} = \alpha + \beta X_{t-1} + \gamma Z_{t} + \epsilon \] \\

Where $CE_{t}$ represents a measure of the number of candidates greater than one per seat, 
$\alpha$ is an intercept, $X_{t-1}$ is a vector of lagged covariates such as prior 
levels of contestation and district size, and $Z_{t}$ is the absolute value of the 
deviation from 50\% of the vote share for the Governor in a recall election aggregated 
to the school district from ward level returns.\footnote{Depending on the data and 
the prevalence of incumbent retirements candidate emergence may need to be measured a 
number of additional ways. \citet{RefWorks:337} discuss a number of possibilities for 
turnover that could be adapted to candidate emergence including: Seats available 
divided by incumbents + challengers minus retired candidates; seats available divided 
by all candidates; or seats available divided by challengers.} Thus, when $Z_{t} = 0$ 
the community is completely divided on the Governor. When $Z_{t}=.5$ the community is 
either united for or against the Governor. Thus, all things equal, we would expect 
$\gamma$ to have a negative value--as $Z_{t}$ increases, the number of challengers, 
$Y_{t}$ should decrease because the community is fairly homogeneous and the school 
board should reflect existing preferences for or against the Governor's reforms. 

\subsection{Turnout}

\subsection{The Impact of Salience and Information on Voter Turnout}

While the equations above can prove useful for providing evidence of various theories 
of voter turnout in non-partisan local elections, they cannot test some theories and 
they do not shed any light on causes of voter turnout. Table \ref{turnoutH2} demonstrates 
how two such models can be explicitly tested by leveraging the exogenous shock of 
the passage of Act 10. To estimate these effects, we estimate an equation like below: \\

\[(6) \hspace{0.4 in} VP_{t} = \alpha + \beta X_{lag} + \gamma W_{t} + \lambda P_{lag} +
      \tau 1- |W_{t} * P_{lag}| + \theta I_{lag} + \psi Ce_{t}+\epsilon \] \\

Here $VP_{t}$ is voter turnout in either the 2011 or 2012 Spring Elections by school 
district in Wisconsin. $\alpha$, $X_{lag}$ remain the same as above though now 
including some measure of prior turnout in the district such as turnout in the 
Gubernatorial election in 2010.  $Z_{t}$ is the absolute value of the deviation 
from 50\% of the vote share for the Governor. $I_{lag}$ representing an indicator 
of the strength of teachers' unions in the district--likely the most salient and 
relevant interest group in the spring elections \citep{RefWorks:320, RefWorks:207, RefWorks:218}.
Voters are unlikely to participate if presented with no choice, so $Ce_{t}$ represents 
a measure of the number of candidates per seat. $W_{t}$ represents the vote share 
for Governor Walker in the recall election--a measure of community support or opposition 
to the Governor's education policy. Thus it would be expected that $\theta$ should 
be positive--strong interest groups should drive political activity. The inclusion 
of partisanship predictors of voter turnout could also be explored here. The 
effects of $\gamma$  and $\lambda$ alone are uninteresting, but the interaction 
of $W_{t} * P_{lag}$ is of great interest. This essentially represents a measure 
of board policy alignment with voter preferences--taking the absolute value simplifies 
interpretation. As such, it should be expected that for large values of $W_{t} * P_{lag}$, 
voter participation would be small as the board policy and the community 
preferences would be aligned. As $W_{t} * P_{lag}$ approaches zero, voter 
participation will increase as predicted by \citep{RefWorks:243}--voters seeing 
dissonance between their preferences and board policy are more likely to vote. 
Thus $\tau$ should be negative.  %Add detail here


\clearpage
\section{Results}



\subsection{Contestation}

To test the effect of the passage of Act 10 on participation in school board elections, 
we fit a simple longitudinal model of school districts. In this model, school district 
elections are treated as repeated measures, nested within the school districts 
themselves. Using this repeated measures framework allows us to estimate cross 
district and within district effects. Act 10 represented a statewide intervention 
in the relative power of school boards in Wisconsin by giving school boards much 
more authority and control over the compensation of teachers and staff, and by 
dramatically restricting the ability of the districts' employees to bargain over 
wages, working conditions, and benefits. This shift in power, in essence, raised 
the stakes for school board elections substantially and should have increased the 
overall contestation of school board elections. 

To evaluate this, a series of models of school board election contestation over 
time are fit to attempt to detect changes in contestation levels relative to the 
levels of contestation observed in school districts prior to the passage of 
Act 10. If levels of contestation increase after the introduction of Act 10, even 
after controlling for the factors that influenced this above, it would be strong 
evidence that the statewide policy lever pulled by Act 10 did spur a response 
from candidates in terms of increasing the attractiveness of school board seats. 

<<walkerDataPrep1, results='asis', echo = FALSE>>=
moddat <- dist_turn[, c("contestSer", "contestMinor", "distid","year", 
                        "balance_member_lag", "turnout",
                        "PerBachelorOrAbove", "VAP_adjLOG", "per_white_all", 
                        "districtwide", "turnoutLag1", "topTicketTurnoutSpring",
                        "OOH_share", "median_incomeLOG", "per65o", 
                        "NonWhitePublicPupilPer", "NonPublicPupilPer", 
                        "teachShareofVoters", "locale2", 
                        "member_delta_per", "GovTwoPartyShareDem_recall",
                        "turnout_overall_werc",
                        "eligvoters_werc", 
                        "loss_werc", "worst_margin_werc", "WERC_elec", 
                        "GovTurnout_recall")]

moddat <- na.omit(moddat)
moddat <- moddat[moddat$year >= 2007,]
moddat <- moddat[order(moddat$distid, moddat$year),]
moddat$treatment <- ifelse(moddat$year > 2010, 1, 0)
moddat$treatment <- factor(moddat$treatment)
moddat$distid <- factor(moddat$distid)
moddat$year <- factor(moddat$year)
moddat <- moddat[order(moddat$distid, moddat$year),]
moddat$turnoutLag1 <- moddat$turnoutLag1 * 100
moddat$districtwide <- factor(moddat$districtwide)
moddat$per_white_all <- moddat$per_white_all * 100
moddat$per65o <- moddat$per65o * 100
moddat$PerBachelorOrAbove <- moddat$PerBachelorOrAbove * 100
moddat$OOH_share <- moddat$OOH_share * 100
# moddat$fallTurnout <- moddat$fallTurnout * 100
moddat$RecallPolarization <- abs(moddat$GovTwoPartyShareDem_recall - 0.5)
moddat$RecallPolarization <- moddat$RecallPolarization * 100
moddat$turnout_overall_werc <- moddat$turnout_overall_werc * 100
moddat$topTicketTurnoutSpring <- moddat$topTicketTurnoutSpring * 100
moddat$turnout <- moddat$turnout * 100
moddat$GovTurnout_recall <- moddat$GovTurnout_recall * 100
moddat$GovTwoPartyShareDem_recall <- moddat$GovTwoPartyShareDem_recall *100
moddat$member_delta_per <- moddat$member_delta_per * 100
moddat$contestSer <- factor(moddat$contestSer)
moddat$contestMinor <- factor(moddat$contestMinor)
moddat$districtwide <- factor(moddat$districtwide)

@

<<walkerplot, fig.cap="Polarization and Turnout Before and After Act 10">>=
qplot(contestMinor, RecallPolarization, data = moddat, geom = 'boxplot') + 
  facet_wrap(~year) +
  scale_y_continuous() +
  theme_dpi()

qplot(RecallPolarization, turnout, data = moddat[moddat$turnout < 80,], 
      color = factor(contestSer)) + 
  facet_wrap(~year) + geom_smooth(method = 'lm', aes(group = 1)) +
  scale_y_continuous() +
  theme_dpi()


# qplot(RecallPolarization, turnoutDiff, data = moddat) + geom_smooth(method = "lm") + 
#   facet_wrap(~year) + scale_x_continuous(label = percent) + 
#   scale_y_continuous(label = percent) +
#   theme_dpi()
@


<<walkerGLM, results='asis'>>=
# Rescale
library(arm)

nums <- sapply(moddat, is.numeric)

for(i in unique(moddat$year)){
  moddat[moddat$year == i, nums] <- 
    apply(moddat[moddat$year == i, nums], 2, rescale)
}
# moddat[, nums] <- apply(moddat[, nums], 2, rescale)

causal1 <- glm(contestMinor ~ turnoutLag1 + VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + 
               per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG +
               districtwide + RecallPolarization*treatment + 
               treatment + turnout_overall_werc, 
              data = moddat, family = 'binomial')

causal2 <- glm(contestMinor ~ turnoutLag1 + VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + 
                 topTicketTurnoutSpring +
               per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG +
               districtwide + RecallPolarization*treatment + 
               treatment + turnout_overall_werc, 
              data = moddat, family = 'binomial')

# causal3 <- glm(contestSer ~ turnoutLag1 + VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + 
#                per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG +
#                districtwide + RecallPolarization*treatment + 
#                treatment + turnout_overall_werc + distid, 
#               data = moddat, family = 'binomial')
# 


apsrtable(causal1, causal2, se = "vcov", label="tab:causmod1", 
          model.names=c("Naive Causal Model", "Overvote Model"), 
          caption = "Model of Causal Impact of Act 10 on School Board Candidacy",
          Sweave=FALSE)

# 
# zed1 <- lmer(turnout ~ turnoutLag2 + fallTurnout + VAP_adj + per_white_all + 
#                per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + PCI + CLOSE + 
#                districtwide + contest + RecallPolarization*treatment + 
#                treatment + turnout_overall_werc + (1|distid), 
#              data = moddat)

@

Table \ref{tab:causmod1} shows good things. 

<<walkerPartialPool, results='asis', echo = FALSE>>=
library(optimx)
ctrl <- glmerControl(optimizer = "optimx", optCtrl = list(method = "nlminb", 
                                                          maxit = 4000))

mixedModCaus <- glmer(contestSer ~ turnoutLag1 + VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + 
               per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG +
               districtwide + RecallPolarization*treatment + turnout_overall_werc +
               treatment + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, family = 'binomial', control = ctrl)

mixedModCaus2 <- glmer(contestMinor ~ turnoutLag1 + VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + 
                        topTicketTurnoutSpring +
               per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG +
               districtwide + RecallPolarization*treatment + 
               treatment + turnout_overall_werc + (1|distid), 
              data = moddat, family = 'binomial', control = ctrl)

apsrtable(mixedModCaus, mixedModCaus2, se = "vcov", label="tab:causME", 
          model.names=c("Serious", "Minor"), 
          caption = "Partial Pooled Model of Causal Impact of Act 10 on School Board Candidacy",
          Sweave=FALSE)


@
\clearpage

\subsection{Turnout}

Using the election of Governor Walker and passage of Act 10 as an exogenous shock that 
increased the relevance and power of school boards, the following models seek to 
test the causal impact on turnout. In Figure \ref{fig:walkerplot} I examine the 
relationship between partisan polarization and turnout before and after the passage 
of Act 10. To test this relationship I restrict the sample to the two spring 
elections prior to the passage of Act 10 -- 2009 and 2010 -- and the two elections 
immediately after the passage of Act 10 -- 2011 and 2012. I code these latter 
years with a dummy variable indicating they are in the post-Act 10 era. The key 
theoretical contribution is that the polarization of the electorate should matter 
more after the passage of Act 10, when party lines for education issues became 
clear. 

<<walkerDataPrep2, results='asis', echo = FALSE>>=
## Grab Walker recall results

moddat <- dist_turn[, c("turnout", "turnoutLag2", "year", "fallTurnout", "VAP_adjLOG", "distid", 
                        "per_white_all", "per65o", "PerBachelorOrAbove", "OOH_share", "median_incomeLOG", 
                        "CLOSE", "districtwide", "contest", "GovTwoPartyShareDem_recall",
                        "teachShareofVoters", "turnout_overall_werc", "eligvoters_werc", 
                        "loss_werc", "worst_margin_werc", "WERC_elec", 
                        "topTicketTurnoutSpring", "turnoutDiff", "GovTurnout_recall")]

moddat <- na.omit(moddat)
moddat <- moddat[moddat$year >= 2007,]
moddat <- moddat[order(moddat$distid, moddat$year),]
moddat$treatment <- ifelse(moddat$year > 2010, 1, 0)
moddat$distid <- factor(moddat$distid)
moddat$year <- factor(moddat$year)
moddat <- moddat[order(moddat$distid, moddat$year),]
moddat$distid <- factor(moddat$distid)
moddat$year <- factor(moddat$year)
moddat$turnout <- moddat$turnout * 100
moddat$districtwide <- factor(moddat$districtwide)
# moddat$turnoutLag2 <- moddat$turnoutLag2 * 100
# moddat$turnoutLag1 <- moddat$turnoutLag1 * 100
# moddat$per_white_all <- moddat$per_white_all * 100
# moddat$per65o <- moddat$per65o * 100
# moddat$PerBachelorOrAbove <- moddat$PerBachelorOrAbove * 100
# moddat$OOH_share <- moddat$OOH_share * 100
# moddat$fallTurnout <- moddat$fallTurnout * 100
# moddat$VAP_adj <- log(moddat$VAP_adj)
# moddat$median_income <- log(moddat$median_income)

centr <- mean(moddat$turnout)
stddev <- sd(moddat$turnout)
moddat$outflag <- (moddat$turnout - centr) / stddev
moddat <- moddat[moddat$outflag <= 5 & moddat$outflag >= -5,]
moddat$RecallPolarization <- abs(moddat$GovTwoPartyShareDem_recall - 0.5)

library(rms)
dd <<- datadist(moddat)
options(datadist="dd")

@

<<walkerplotT, fig.cap="Polarization and Turnout Before and After Act 10">>=
qplot(RecallPolarization, turnout/100, data = moddat) + geom_smooth(method = "lm") + 
  facet_wrap(~year) + scale_x_continuous(label = percent) + 
  scale_y_continuous(label = percent) +
  theme_dpi()

# qplot(RecallPolarization, turnoutDiff, data = moddat) + geom_smooth(method = "lm") + 
#   facet_wrap(~year) + scale_x_continuous(label = percent) + 
#   scale_y_continuous(label = percent) +
#   theme_dpi()
@


<<walkerOLSmodT, results='asis'>>=
causal1 <- ols(turnout ~ turnoutLag2 + fallTurnout + VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + 
               per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + CLOSE + 
               districtwide + contest + RecallPolarization*treatment + 
               treatment + turnout_overall_werc, 
              data = moddat, x = TRUE, y = TRUE)

causal1$se <- sqrt(diag(vcov(robcov(causal1, cluster = moddat$distid))))

causal2 <- ols(turnout ~ turnoutLag2 + fallTurnout + VAP_adjLOG + 
                 per_white_all + topTicketTurnoutSpring +
               per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + CLOSE + 
               districtwide + contest + RecallPolarization*treatment + 
               treatment + turnout_overall_werc, 
              data = moddat, x = TRUE, y = TRUE)

causal2$se <- sqrt(diag(vcov(robcov(causal2, cluster = moddat$distid))))


apsrtable(causal1, causal2, se = "robust", label="tab:causmod1", 
          model.names=c("Naive Causal Model", "Overvote Model"), 
          caption = "Model of Causal Impact of Act 10 on School Board Elections",
          Sweave=FALSE)

# 
# zed1 <- lmer(turnout ~ turnoutLag2 + fallTurnout + VAP_adj + per_white_all + 
#                per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_income + CLOSE + 
#                districtwide + contest + RecallPolarization*treatment + 
#                treatment + turnout_overall_werc + (1|distid), 
#              data = moddat)

@

Table \ref{tab:causmod1} shows good things. 

<<walkerPartialPoolT, results='asis', echo = FALSE>>=
mixedModCaus <- lmer(turnout ~turnoutLag2 + fallTurnout + VAP_adjLOG + per_white_all + 
               per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + CLOSE + 
               districtwide + contest + RecallPolarization*treatment + 
               treatment + turnout_overall_werc + (1|distid) + (1|year), 
              data = moddat)

mixedModCaus2 <- lmer(turnoutDiff*100 ~ turnoutLag2 + fallTurnout + VAP_adjLOG + 
                 per_white_all + topTicketTurnoutSpring +
               per65o + PerBachelorOrAbove + OOH_share + median_incomeLOG + CLOSE + 
               districtwide + contest + RecallPolarization*treatment + 
               treatment + turnout_overall_werc + (1|distid) + (1|year), 
              data = moddat)
tabnote <- "Model includes both year and district random effects modeled as intercepts."

apsrtable(mixedModCaus, mixedModCaus2, caption = "Partial Pooling Model of Post-Election Polarization",
          model.names = c("Turnout", "Rolloff"),
            label = "tab:causModme", notes = list(tabnote, stars.note), 
          Sweave = FALSE)
@

Table \ref{tab:causModme} shows the results here.

